n University of Life Sciences, {NMBU}, Norway{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{26}}$Earth System Science Centre ({CCST}), National Institute for Space Research ({INPE}), José dos Campos, {SP}, Brazil{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{27}}$Nicholas School of the Environment, Duke University,Durham, {NC} U.S.A{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{28}}${NASA} {GSFC}, Biospheric Sciences Laboratory, Greenbelt, {MD}, {USA}{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{29}}$Carrera 5 No 14-05, Cundinamarca, Colombia{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{30}}$World Wide Fund for Nature({WWF}) Germany Biodiversity Unit, Berlin, Germany{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{31}}$Department of Environmental Sciences, University of Virginia, Charlottesville, Virginia, {USA}{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{32}}$Faculty of Science, Universiti Brunei Darussalam, Gadong, Brunei{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{33}}$Department of Geographical Sciences, University of Maryland, College Park, {MD}, {USA}{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{34}}$International Centre for Integrated Mountain Development, Khumaltar, Lalitpur, Kathmandu, Nepal{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{35}}$Universidade Regional de Blumenau – Depto. de Engenharia Florestal, R. São Paulo, {SC} – Brasil.{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{36}}$Agence Nationale des Parcs Nationaux, Libreville, Gabon, Ministère de la Forêt, de la Mer, de l'Environnement, Chargé du Plan Climat, Libreville, Gabon.{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{37}}$United States Forest Service, Northern Research Station–Durham, Durham, {NH}, {USA}.{\textless}/em{\textgreater} {\textless}em{\textgreater}$^{\textrm{38}}$Department of Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences, University of California, Los Angeles, California, {USA}{\textless}/em{\textgreater} Classification: Physical sciences, environmental sciences Keywords: forest biomass, forest height, carbon cycle, microwave and optical imaging, error propagation, maximum entropy Corresponding Author: Sassan Saatchi Jet Propulsion Laboratory California Institute of Technology 4800 Oak Grove Drive Pasadena, {CA} 91109 Email: Saatchi@jpl.nasa.gov Tel: +1-818-354-1051 {\textless}strong{\textgreater}Abstract{\textless}/strong{\textgreater} Carbon emissions from forest disturbance are estimated by the area of disturbance multiplied by the emission factors. The area of disturbance is routinely quantified by high-resolution satellite observations, while emission factors are inferred from forest inventory data at national or global scales. This discrepancy between the scale of disturbance and emission factors is hypothesized to introduce large bias in estimates of annual carbon emissions. To test the hypothesis, we used the 30-m global forest cover change to show that on average 80\% of disturbances are less than 10-ha in size and 75\% of the time occur within 1-km of past disturbances; together pointing to the optimum scale for quantifying emission factors. Using systematic inventory of forest structure from ground, air, and space and satellite imagery, we map global vegetation live biomass at 100-m spatial resolution (1-ha) with a combined model-based and spatial machine learning estimators. We found large spatial heterogeneity of carbon storage at 1-ha scale related to impacts of disturbance and recovery processes and natural edaphic variations amounting to 428±64 {PgC} (341±51 {PgC} above, 87±13 {PgC} below) partitioned into 328 {PgC} in forests and 100 {PgC} in savannas and shrublands. We verified the hypothesis by showing that there was up to 30\% bias from underestimating emissions when the scale of emission factors increased and the bias varied geographically with forest types and disturbance regimes. Our results show that fine-scale mapping of biomass carbon density is essential in reducing the uncertainty of carbon emissions and removals from terrestrial ecosystems.},
